# {{ site_name }}

YOURLS site for <{{ site_url }}>, managed with [yourls-manager](https://github.com/RubenFixit/yourls-manager) (`ym`) and generated from [yourls-site-template](https://github.com/RubenFixit/yourls-site-template).

The complete site configuration lives in this repo. Secrets live only on the server.
{%- if repo_url %} Repository: <{{ repo_url }}>.{% endif %}

## Layout

| Path | Owner | What |
|---|---|---|
| `site.toml` | you | The site definition: core version, plugins, deploy targets. |
| `yourls.lock.json` | `ym` | Exact core/plugin commits and tree hashes. Commit it. |
| `yourls/` | `ym` | Vendored YOURLS core + plugins. Never hand-edit; `ym doctor --local` shows drift. |
| `overlay/` | template + you | Copied over `yourls/` at build time (config shim, `health.php`, `.htaccess`, branding plugin, local plugin patches). |
| `server/config.example.php` | template | Starting point for the server-local config. |
| `tests/redirects.toml` | you | Redirect expectations checked by `ym verify`. |
{%- if ci_provider == 'github' %}
| `.github/` | template | GitHub Actions: deploy on tag, weekly update PRs{% if release_artifact %}, publish release artifacts{% endif %}; Dependabot. |
{%- elif ci_provider == 'gitlab' %}
| `.gitlab-ci.yml` | template | GitLab CI: deploy on tag, weekly update merge requests{% if release_artifact %}, publish release artifacts{% endif %}. |
{%- endif %}
| `adopt-review/` | `ym adopt` | Only after adopting an existing install: report + files to merge by hand. Never deployed. |

Files marked *template* are updated by `ym template update` (local edits are merged, conflicts are marked). `site.toml`, `tests/redirects.toml` and the branding assets are never touched by updates.

Full documentation: the [yourls-manager README](https://github.com/RubenFixit/yourls-manager#readme). Commands below use the globally installed `ym` (`uv tool install git+https://github.com/RubenFixit/yourls-manager`). `uv run ym ...` runs the version pinned in `pyproject.toml` instead, which is what CI uses.

## Setup

If this repo was created by `ym new` / `ym init`, YOURLS core and plugins are already vendored. (After a raw `copier copy`, run `ym core` and `ym plugin sync` first.)

```sh
ym target config prod                # server-local config: DB, cookie key, admin user (0600, never in Git)
{%- if deploy_type == 'sftp' %}
ym deploy prod --dry-run             # build + show what would be uploaded/deleted, change nothing
{%- else %}
ym deploy prod --dry-run             # build + show the rsync changes, change nothing
{%- endif %}
ym deploy prod
```

The server-local config lives at `<parent of web root>/yourls-config/{{ site_slug }}.php`. The shim at `overlay/user/config.php` loads it, and `YOURLS_CONFIG_FILE` can point elsewhere. Passwords are read from `YM_DB_PASSWORD` / `YM_ADMIN_PASSWORD` or prompted for. To edit the file by hand, start from `server/config.example.php` (instructions inside).

{% if deploy_type == 'sftp' -%}
The `prod` target deploys over **SFTP** (no shell needed). ym logs in with an SSH key (`ym target login prod`) or the password in `YM_PROD_PASSWORD` (asked on a terminal otherwise), and keeps a manifest of deployed files in `<parent of web root>/.yourls-manager/{{ site_slug }}/`: it only ever deletes files it deployed itself. There is no PHP on the server for ym, so:

- ym does **not** back up the database. Use your host's backup tool before deploying.
- First deploy to an empty database: open `{{ site_url }}/admin/install.php` once to create the tables. After a core update, ym tells you when to open `{{ site_url }}/admin/upgrade.php`.
- Plugins: activate exactly the ones marked `active = true` in `site.toml` at `{{ site_url }}/admin/plugins.php`. Every deploy prints the list.
{%- else -%}
First deploy to an empty database: open `{{ site_url }}/admin/install.php` once to create the tables, then run `ym plugin apply prod`. Later deploys run DB upgrades and plugin activation automatically.
{%- endif %}

Taking over a YOURLS install that already exists? Run `ym adopt --from-target prod`, review `adopt-review/REPORT.md`, then run `ym target config prod --from-existing` and deploy (see the yourls-manager README).

## Day-to-day

```sh
ym doctor                            # read-only health check: tools, repo, every target
ym doctor --local                    # offline: lock, drift, uncommitted changes
ym update --check                    # what has upstream updates (exit 10 = updates)
{%- if deploy_type == 'sftp' %}
ym deploy prod --dry-run             # build + show what would be uploaded/deleted, change nothing
ym deploy prod                       # build, upload changes over SFTP, check install/upgrade, verify
ym deploy prod --verify-remote       # also re-upload files someone changed on the server
{%- else %}
ym deploy prod --dry-run             # build + show the rsync changes, change nothing
ym deploy prod                       # build, back up DB, rsync, upgrade, plugins, verify
{%- endif %}
ym verify prod                       # health check + tests/redirects.toml
```

### Plugins

```sh
ym plugin search redirect            # search the awesome-yourls catalog
ym plugin add "404 If Not Found"     # by catalog name, or a git / GitHub project URL
ym plugin list --check               # version, source, locked commit, upstream updates
ym plugin disable NAME               # or enable; sets `active` in site.toml
ym plugin remove NAME                # drops its [[plugins]] table and vendored copy
```

To add one:

1. `ym plugin add NAME|URL` appends a `[[plugins]]` table to `site.toml` and vendors the plugin into `yourls/user/plugins/<name>/`, locking the commit. (By hand: add the table with `name`, `repo`, optional `ref`/`path`/`active`, then `ym plugin sync`.)
2. Review what it shows (plugin header, requirements).
{%- if deploy_type == 'sftp' %}
3. Commit `site.toml`, `yourls.lock.json`, `yourls/`; deploy. Then activate it at `{{ site_url }}/admin/plugins.php` (ym can't over SFTP; the deploy lists the plugins).
{%- else %}
3. Commit `site.toml`, `yourls.lock.json`, `yourls/`; deploy. Deploy activates exactly the plugins marked `active = true`.
{%- endif %}

{% if deploy_type == 'sftp' -%}
Before removing a plugin, deactivate it at `{{ site_url }}/admin/plugins.php`; the next deploy deletes its files. Its data (options, tables) stays in the database.
{%- else -%}
The next deploy deactivates a removed plugin and deletes its files; its data (options, tables) stays in the database.
{%- endif %}
Patch a vendored plugin by putting the changed files under `overlay/user/plugins/<name>/` (overlay wins at build time).

### Update YOURLS core and plugins

```sh
ym update --commit                   # core to the latest release, plugins to newest commits,
                                     # build, and commit with a summary of the changes
git push
ym release --push                    # tag v<yourls>-<rev> and deploy it (see below)
```
{% if ci_provider == 'github' %}
The `check-updates` workflow does this weekly and opens a pull request.
{% elif ci_provider == 'gitlab' %}
The `check-updates` CI job does this on a pipeline schedule and opens a merge request (see [GitLab CI](#gitlab-ci)).
{% endif %}
### Releases

Release tags are `v<YOURLS version>-<revision>`, e.g. `v1.10.6-1`, `v1.10.6-2`; the revision counts site changes and restarts at 1 when YOURLS is updated. Release candidates add `-rc.<n>` (`v1.10.6-3-rc.1`).

```sh
ym release --rc --push        # v1.10.6-3-rc.1 -> deploys to staging (if defined)
ym release --rc --push        # after fixes: v1.10.6-3-rc.2
ym release promote --push     # v1.10.6-3 on the rc's commit -> deploys to prod
ym release --push             # no staging? tag the next final release directly
ym release list               # releases and where each one is deployed
```

The tag message holds release notes built from `yourls.lock.json` (YOURLS and plugin changes) and the commit log. Each target's `accept` setting decides what it takes: prod has `accept = "release"` (only final release tags, clean tree; `--force` overrides and is recorded), other targets default to `"any"`. `ym deploy` refuses uncommitted changes everywhere unless you pass `--allow-dirty`, and after each deploy moves the local branch `deployed/<target>` to the deployed commit (`[release] push_markers = true` pushes it).

{% if release_artifact %}### Release artifacts

Every release is also published as an archive of the built site, for systems that package and deploy it themselves. `[artifact]` in `site.toml` sets the formats and file names; `ym artifact build` builds them into `dist-artifacts/`:

```sh
ym release --push                    # tag the release{% if ci_provider != 'none' %}; CI publishes its artifacts{% endif %}
ym artifact build                    # the same files locally (HEAD must carry a release tag)
ym artifact build --ref v1.10.6-1    # rebuild an older release: identical bytes
```

| File | Contents |
|---|---|
| `{{ site_slug }}-<version>.tar.gz` | The built web root (`yourls/` + `overlay/`), no top-level directory. Rebuilding a tag gives byte-identical archives. |
| `{{ site_slug }}-<version>.tar.gz.sha256` | `sha256sum` format. |
| `{{ site_slug }}-<version>.json` | Release metadata: site, version, tag, rc, commit, locked core and plugin commits, yourls-manager version, file hashes. |

`<version>` is the tag without the `v` (`1.10.6-2`, `1.10.6-3-rc.1`).
{%- if ci_provider == 'github' %} The `publish` workflow attaches the files to a GitHub release for every release tag (rc tags as pre-releases).
{%- elif ci_provider == 'gitlab' %} The `publish` CI job uploads them to the project's Generic Package Registry (package `{{ site_slug }}`, version `<version>`) and creates a release linking them; turn on *Settings → Packages and registries → Do not allow duplicate generic packages* so a published version can't be replaced.
{%- else %} This site has no CI, so publish the files yourself (or add CI with `ym template update --data ci_provider=github`).
{%- endif %}

#### Consuming the artifact

{%- set rel_base = repo_url ~ '/releases/download' if repo_url else 'https://github.com/<owner>/<repo>/releases/download' %}
{%- if repo_url and '://' in repo_url %}
{%-   set host = repo_url.split('://')[1].split('/', 1)[0] %}
{%-   set path = repo_url.split('://')[1].split('/', 1)[1] %}
{%-   set pkg_base = repo_url.split('://')[0] ~ '://' ~ host ~ '/api/v4/projects/' ~ path | replace('/', '%2F') ~ '/packages/generic/' ~ site_slug %}
{%- else %}
{%-   set pkg_base = 'https://<gitlab host>/api/v4/projects/<project id or url-encoded path>/packages/generic/' ~ site_slug %}
{%- endif %}

1. Pin a release: its version, the archive URL and the archive's sha256 (from the `.sha256` file or the `.json`).
{%- if ci_provider == 'github' %}
   URL: `{{ rel_base }}/v<version>/{{ site_slug }}-<version>.tar.gz`
{%- elif ci_provider == 'gitlab' %}
   URL: `{{ pkg_base }}/<version>/{{ site_slug }}-<version>.tar.gz` (private projects: send a `PRIVATE-TOKEN`, `DEPLOY-TOKEN` or `JOB-TOKEN` header).
{%- endif %}
2. Download it and verify the hash before using it (`sha256sum -c {{ site_slug }}-<version>.tar.gz.sha256`).
3. Extract it into an empty web root (`tar -xzf ... -C <web root>`); the archive root is the web root.
4. Provide the server-local config: put it at `<parent of web root>/yourls-config/{{ site_slug }}.php`, or point the environment variable `YOURLS_CONFIG_FILE` at it (the shim in `user/config.php` reads that first). Start from `server/config.example.php`. The archive never contains secrets.
5. On a new or upgraded YOURLS version, open `/admin/install.php` or `/admin/upgrade.php` once, and activate the plugins marked `active = true` in `site.toml` (the `.json` lists the vendored ones).

{% endif %}{% if ci_provider == 'github' %}### GitHub (optional)

Deploys run from your machine; a Git remote is optional. To keep the site on GitHub (needs the [GitHub CLI](https://cli.github.com)):

```sh
ym repo create                       # private repo as origin, pushes the current branch
```

To deploy from GitHub Actions instead of your machine:

```sh
ym ci setup prod                     # environment, deploy key, host keys; sets ci = true
git commit -m "Deploy from GitHub Actions" site.toml && git push
ym ci status                         # check the environments and secrets
```

Then pushing a release tag runs `.github/workflows/deploy.yml`: final tags deploy `prod` in the `production` environment, `-rc.<n>` tags deploy `staging` in the `staging` environment. Manual runs can pick any target. Only targets with `ci = true` in site.toml are deployed; the others are skipped with a notice. The environments hold the secrets `DEPLOY_SSH_KEY` (private key authorized on the host) and `DEPLOY_KNOWN_HOSTS` (the host's keys).

{% elif ci_provider == 'gitlab' %}### GitLab CI

`.gitlab-ci.yml` runs yourls-manager in the `ghcr.io/astral-sh/uv` image (Docker executor). Deploys run from your machine until you opt a target in with `ci = true` in `site.toml`. `ym repo create` and `ym ci` are GitHub-only, so set GitLab up by hand:

1. Push the repo to a GitLab project and protect the tags `v*` (*Settings → Repository → Protected tags*), so protected variables reach release-tag pipelines.
2. Create the environments `production` (target `prod`) and `staging` (every other target) under *Operate → Environments*.
3. For each environment, add two **File**-type, protected CI/CD variables scoped to it (*Settings → CI/CD → Variables*):
   - `DEPLOY_SSH_KEY`: a private key (e.g. `ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -N "" -C ym-ci-{{ site_slug }}`) whose public half is in `~/.ssh/authorized_keys` on the host. Use a key only CI has.
   - `DEPLOY_KNOWN_HOSTS`: the host's keys, `ssh-keyscan -p <port> <host>`; compare the fingerprints with `ssh-keygen -lf` against a trusted source.
4. Set `ci = true` on the target in `site.toml`, commit and push.
5. Weekly updates: add a pipeline schedule (*Build → Pipeline schedules*) and a masked variable `YM_UPDATE_TOKEN` holding a project access token with the `write_repository` scope (Developer role). The `check-updates` job pushes `ym/upstream-updates` and opens a merge request.

Pushing a release tag then deploys: final tags deploy `prod` in `production`, `-rc.<n>` tags deploy `staging` in `staging`. To deploy by hand from CI, run a pipeline (*Build → Pipelines → Run pipeline*) with the variable `TARGET=<name>`. Targets without `ci = true` are skipped with a message.

{% else %}### CI

This site has no CI files: deploys and update checks run from your machine (`ym deploy`, `ym update --check`). Add GitHub Actions or GitLab CI later with `ym template update --data ci_provider=github` (or `gitlab`).

{% endif %}### Roll back

Rollback is manual and Git-based:

1. Check out the last good release and deploy it: `git checkout v<previous> && ym deploy prod`{% if ci_provider == 'github' %} (or run the deploy workflow on that tag){% elif ci_provider == 'gitlab' %} (or run a pipeline on that tag with `TARGET=prod`){% endif %}.
{%- if deploy_type == 'sftp' %}
2. If a DB upgrade or bad data needs undoing, restore the database from your host's backups (SFTP deploys don't dump the database).
{%- else %}
2. If a DB upgrade or bad data needs undoing, restore the dump taken before the bad deploy from the backup dir on the server (default `<parent of web root>/yourls-backups/{{ site_slug }}/`): `gunzip -c <dump>.sql.gz | mysql --defaults-extra-file=<file with credentials> <db>`.
{%- endif %}

### Update the tooling

- Template files (CI files, shim, `.htaccess`, ...): `ym template update` (needs a clean Git tree), then review the diff and commit. Answers are stored in `.copier-answers.yml`. Don't edit it by hand; change an answer with `--data`, e.g. `ym template update --data ci_provider=gitlab` (switches the CI files) or `--data release_artifact=true` (adds the publish job; add the `[artifact]` table to `site.toml` yourself, since updates never touch that file).
- yourls-manager pin used by CI: `ym template update --manager-version vX.Y.Z` (bumps `pyproject.toml` and runs `uv lock`), then review and commit.
- Your global `ym`: `uv tool upgrade yourls-manager`.
